description:
The reinitializer function, which destroys the bridged token’s initial supply, carries no caller authorization, while the two supply-changing functions beside it are both role-gated.

```solidity
function reinitialize(address _recipient) external reinitializer(2) {
    // Burn initial supply from recipient
    _burn(_recipient, INITIAL_SUPPLY);

    // Ensure total supply is zero after upgrade
    /// @dev This is the bridged token, so its supply should start at 0
    assert(totalSupply() == 0);
}

/// @inheritdoc IMintableBurnable
function mint(address _to, uint256 _amount) external onlyRole(MINTER_ROLE) returns
(bool success) {
    _mint(_to, _amount);
    success = true;
}
```

*Figure 1.1: The reinitializer function accepts any caller and any recipient, unlike the role-gated mint function beside it. (token/src/BridgedGensynToken.sol#L28–L41)*

OpenZeppelin’s reinitializer modifier enforces only the initialization version and performs no caller-authorization check. Consequently, any account can call `reinitialize` on a proxy that has not yet consumed version two. The call succeeds only if `_recipient` holds `INITIAL_SUPPLY`, and burning that amount reduces `totalSupply()` to zero; otherwise, `_burn` or the trailing assertion reverts and rolls back the version change.

In the deployment produced by the bridged-token script, `initialize` assigns the entire initial supply to `0x000…dEaD`. An attacker can therefore successfully call `reinitialize` only by specifying that address, which produces the same zero-supply state intended by the deployer. The defect becomes exploitable if a future upgrade points a compatible proxy at this implementation, while version two remains unused and the entire initial supply is held by a live address: any account could specify that address and irreversibly burn its tokens.

