The same issue also occurs in the `initialize(...)` instruction. Calling the `add_target(...)` function updates `updated_by` and `updated_at`, but they have already been updated beforehand.

recommendation:
It is recommended not to redundantly update `updated_by` and `updated_at` before and after calling the `add_target(...)` and `remove_target(...)` functions.

**Proof of Concept:**
```rust
pub fn handler(ctx: Context<Remove>, target: Pubkey) -> Result<()> {
    //...
    admin.remove_target(&target, caller_admin.key());
    admin.updated_at = Clock::get()?.unix_timestamp as u64;
    admin.updated_by = caller_admin.key();

    Ok(())
}
```

```rust
pub fn handler(...) -> Result<()> {
    //...
    platform_admin.updated_at = current_time;
    platform_admin.updated_by = authority.key();
    //...
    platform_admin.add_target(platform_config.key(), authority.key());
    //...
}
```

description:
When the number of proposal approvers reaches the threshold, the proposal can be executed early, and `early_execution_approved` is set to true.

```rust
pub fn handler(ctx: Context<ApproveTimeLock>) -> Result<bool> {
    //...
    if threshold_reached {
        // Set ready_at to current time to allow immediate execution
        let current_time = Clock::get()?.unix_timestamp;
        time_lock.ready_at = current_time;
        time_lock.early_execution_approved = true;
        //...
    }
}
```

The `time_lock` account defines a `TimeLockStatus::Ready` state, but when a proposal can be executed early, the `time_lock` account is not set to this state.

recommendation:
It is recommended to either remove the `TimeLockStatus::Ready` state or set the `time_lock` account to `TimeLockStatus::Ready` when a proposal reaches the threshold, and account for the `TimeLockStatus::Ready` state in `can_execute(...)`.

**Proof of Concept:**
```rust
pub fn handler(ctx: Context<ApproveTimeLock>) -> Result<bool> {
    //...
